# An engine withdrawal is recorded before what it causes ([RFCS.7a.4]). See
# fixtures/126_expiry_consequences.ds.
#
#   pump 1   Gate blocks `ratio`'s `no Gate`, so its join on Q never runs;
#            `keep` emits Held logically from Gate
#   pump 2   the hour has passed and the sweep expires Gate (TTL_EXPIRED).
#            Its removal releases `ratio`'s negated pattern, so the join
#            `(p.n // q.d) > 0` runs against Q d=0 and traps DIVIDE_BY_ZERO;
#            and Held's only justifier is gone, so Held is withdrawn
#            (JUSTIFIER_ENDED)
#
# Pump 2 records Gate's `retract` first, then the fault, then Held's
# `retract`, then the fault fact the drain asserts. RFCS.7a.4 requires the
# first; of the two consequences it admits either order, and this scenario
# holds the runtimes to the one they share (the network re-evaluates during
# the removal, truth maintenance runs after it). The vector
# RFCS.7a.4_expiry_withdraw_precedes_its_consequences pins the sequence
# against the clause.
assert Gate g=1
assert P entity_id=p n=7
assert Q entity_id=zero d=0
pump
advance 3600000000001
pump
